Course.start({"num":2,"unit":1,"slug":"m02-acentos","minutes":40,"title":"Las sílabas y los acentos","subtitle":"Stress, written accent marks, and ¿ ¡","lede":"Every Spanish word has one strong syllable. Two simple rules tell you where it is, and the accent mark (´) tells you when a word breaks the rules.","recap":"Last time you learned the 5 vowel sounds, the names of the 27 letters, and the letters that surprise English speakers, like the silent **h** and the rolled **rr**.","context":{"title":"Una pregunta al revés","place":"Madrid, España","text":"The **Real Academia Española** (Royal Spanish Academy), or **RAE**, was founded in Madrid in 1713 to describe and guide the Spanish language. Its motto is *Limpia, fija y da esplendor*: \"It cleans, sets and gives splendor.\"\n\nSpanish had a problem that English doesn't: a question can look exactly like a statement. {{Tienes un perro.}} means *You have a dog.* {{¿Tienes un perro?}} means *Do you have a dog?* The only difference is your voice. When you read a long sentence aloud, you might not know it's a question until the very end, and by then it's too late to raise your voice.\n\nSo in **1754**, the RAE's book on spelling recommended something new: an **upside-down question mark** at the *start* of a question. The upside-down exclamation mark followed. Today Spanish is the only major language that uses **¿** and **¡**, and they warn every reader in advance: {{¡Atención! ¿Estás listo?}}"},"learn":[{"title":"Two rules for stress","text":"Every word has one syllable that is said louder and longer. Hyphens below show the syllables, and the **bold** one is stressed.\n\n- **Rule 1:** If a word ends in a **vowel**, **n** or **s**, the stress falls on the **second-to-last** syllable.\n- **Rule 2:** If a word ends in **any other consonant**, the stress falls on the **last** syllable.","table":{"head":["Rule 1: ends in vowel, n or s","Rule 2: ends in another consonant"],"rows":[["{{**ca**-sa|casa}}","{{re-**loj**|reloj}}"],["{{e-**xa**-men|examen}}","{{pa-**pel**|papel}}"],["{{za-**pa**-tos|zapatos}}","{{ciu-**dad**|ciudad}}"],["{{**ha**-blan|hablan}}","{{ha-**blar**|hablar}}"]]}},{"title":"The accent mark means \"this word breaks the rule\"","text":"When a word's stress doesn't follow the two rules, Spanish writes an **accent mark** (*tilde*) on the stressed vowel. The mark always slants up to the right: **á é í ó ú**.\n\nWords stressed on the third-to-last syllable *always* have one.","table":{"head":["The word…","Examples"],"rows":[["ends in a vowel, n or s, but the stress is on the **last** syllable","{{ca-**fé**|café}} · {{can-**ción**|canción}} · {{in-**glés**|inglés}} · {{a-**quí**|aquí}}"],["ends in another consonant, but the stress is **not** on the last syllable","{{**lá**-piz|lápiz}} · {{**ár**-bol|árbol}} · {{**fá**-cil|fácil}}"],["is stressed on the **third-to-last** syllable","{{**mú**-si-ca|música}} · {{**sá**-ba-do|sábado}} · {{**plá**-ta-no|plátano}}"]]},"tip":"Leaving off an accent mark is a spelling mistake in Spanish, just like leaving out a letter in English. Use the yellow **á é í ó ú** keys."},{"title":"Accents that change the meaning","text":"Some short words are spelled the same except for the accent mark, and the mark changes the meaning. Questions and exclamations also need **¿ ?** and **¡ !** around them.","table":{"head":["With accent","Without accent"],"rows":[["{{tú}} you","{{tu}} your"],["{{él}} he","{{el}} the"],["{{sí}} yes","{{si}} if"],["{{mí}} me (after *para*, *a*…)","{{mi}} my"],["{{qué}} what? · {{cómo}} how?","{{que}} that · {{como}} like, as"]]},"examples":[["Tú tienes tu libro.","You have your book."],["Él tiene el libro.","He has the book."],["¿Cómo estás?","How are you?"],["¡Qué bien!","How great!"]]}],"tasks":[{"id":"t1","type":"accent","title":"Where does the accent go?","intro":"Say each word out loud (tap 🔊 after you check). Tap the vowel that needs an accent mark, or tap **No accent** if the word follows the rules.","pick":10,"items":["lápiz","canción","mesa","reloj","árbol","examen","música","papel","café","joven","sábado","inglés","ciudad","fácil"],"hints":["First, how does the word end: a vowel, **n** or **s**, or another consonant?","Then, where is the stress when you say it? If that matches the rule, there's **no accent**.","Example: *lapiz* ends in **z**, so Rule 2 says stress the last syllable, *la-PIZ*. But we say *LA-piz*, so it needs an accent: **lápiz**."]},{"id":"t2","type":"listen","title":"Hear the stress","intro":"These words are spelled almost the same. Only the stress is different. Which one do you hear?","questions":[{"say":"papá","keepOrder":true,"choices":[["**pa**-pa (potato)",false,"Listen for which syllable is louder."],["pa-**pá** (dad)",true,"The stress is on the last syllable, so it needs an accent: **papá**."]]},{"say":"papa","keepOrder":true,"choices":[["**pa**-pa (potato)",true,"Ends in a vowel, stress on the second-to-last: no accent."],["pa-**pá** (dad)",false]]},{"say":"está","keepOrder":true,"choices":[["**es**-ta (this)",false],["es-**tá** (is)",true,"**Está** has the stress at the end."]]},{"say":"término","keepOrder":true,"choices":[["**tér**-mi-no (term, end)",true,"Third-to-last: always an accent."],["ter-**mi**-no (I finish)",false],["ter-mi-**nó** (he finished)",false]]},{"say":"terminó","keepOrder":true,"choices":[["**tér**-mi-no (term, end)",false],["ter-**mi**-no (I finish)",false],["ter-mi-**nó** (he finished)",true,"Stress on the last syllable of a word ending in a vowel: accent."]]}],"hints":["Tap 🐢 to hear it slowly, and clap on the loudest syllable.","The accent mark is always on the syllable that's stressed."]},{"id":"t3","type":"select","title":"With or without the accent?","intro":"Choose the right word for each sentence.","items":[{"s":"¿{Tú|Tu} hablas español?","en":"Do you speak Spanish?","why":"*You* (the person) is **tú**."},{"s":"{Tu|Tú} libro está en la mesa.","en":"Your book is on the table.","why":"*Your* is **tu**, no accent."},{"s":"{Él|El} es mi amigo.","en":"He is my friend."},{"s":"{El|Él} gato es negro.","en":"The cat is black."},{"s":"{Sí|Si}, me gusta mucho.","en":"Yes, I like it a lot."},{"s":"{Si|Sí} llueve, no voy.","en":"If it rains, I'm not going."},{"s":"El regalo es para {mí|mi}.","en":"The gift is for me."},{"s":"{Mi|Mí} casa es pequeña.","en":"My house is small."}],"hints":["The person words have the accent: **tú** (you), **él** (he), **mí** (me).","**Sí** (yes) has an accent; **si** (if) doesn't."]},{"id":"t4","type":"sort","title":"Which rule?","intro":"Tap 🔊 to hear each word. Does it follow Rule 1, follow Rule 2, or break the rules (and have an accent mark)?","cats":["Rule 1","Rule 2","Breaks the rule"],"speak":true,"items":[["casa",0],["zapatos",0],["hablan",0],["examen",0],["reloj",1],["papel",1],["ciudad",1],["hablar",1],["lápiz",2],["café",2],["canción",2],["música",2]],"hints":["Any word with an accent mark breaks the rule.","No accent? Look at the last letter: vowel, **n** or **s** is Rule 1; any other consonant is Rule 2."]},{"id":"t5","type":"errors","title":"Fix the spelling","intro":"Each sentence has one word with a missing accent mark or missing **¿**. Tap the word, then type it correctly.","items":[{"s":"Como te llamas?","bad":"Como","fix":"¿Cómo","why":"Questions start with **¿**, and question words have an accent."},{"s":"Mi hermano habla ingles.","bad":"ingles","fix":"inglés"},{"s":"El examen es el sabado.","bad":"sabado","fix":"sábado"},{"s":"¿Dónde esta la biblioteca?","bad":"esta","fix":"está","why":"*Está* (is) is stressed on the last syllable."},{"s":"Me gusta la musica.","bad":"musica","fix":"música"}],"hints":["Read each word out loud. Does its stress follow the rules?","In sentence 1, one word needs both **¿** and an accent mark. Type the ¿ with the yellow key."]},{"id":"t6","type":"dictation","title":"Listen and write","intro":"Write what you hear, with every accent mark. You don't need to type ¿ ¡ or capital letters.","pick":5,"items":["¿Cómo estás?","el lápiz","la canción","Sí, es mi café.","¡Qué bien!","el sábado","Tú y él."],"hints":["Listen for the stressed syllable in each word, then decide if it breaks the rules.","*Qué* and *cómo* in questions and exclamations always have an accent."]},{"id":"s1","type":"speak","title":"Stress it","intro":"Say each word, putting the stress on the bold syllable. Then record and compare.","items":[{"s":"música","en":"MÚ-si-ca"},{"s":"canción","en":"can-CIÓN"},{"s":"examen","en":"e-XA-men"},{"s":"ciudad","en":"ciu-DAD"}]}],"build":{"heading":"Preguntas y exclamaciones","prompt":"Imagine you're meeting a new student. Write **at least 4 questions** and **2 exclamations** you might say. Use **¿ ?** and **¡ !** correctly, and include at least 3 words with accent marks. You can use the phrases below.\n\n{{¿Cómo estás?}} · {{¿Qué tal?}} · {{¿Cómo te llamas?}} · {{¿Dónde está la clase?}} · {{¿Tú hablas inglés?}} · {{¡Qué bien!}} · {{¡Hola!}} · {{¡Mucho gusto!}} · {{¡Adiós!}}","model":"{{¡Hola!}} {{¿Cómo te llamas?}} {{¿Cómo estás?}} {{¿Tú hablas inglés?}} {{¿Dónde está la clase de música?}} {{¡Qué bien!}} {{¡Adiós!}}","placeholder":"¡Hola! ¿Cómo…","reqs":[{"t":"At least 4 questions with **¿ ?**","questions":4},{"t":"At least 2 exclamations with **¡ !**","re":"¡[^!]+!","min":2},{"t":"At least 3 words with an accent mark","re":"\\p{L}*[áéíóú]\\p{L}*","min":3}]},"reflect":{"q":"A tiny mark can change *tu* (your) into *tú* (you), or a statement into a question. Think of a time when a small detail (a word, a tone of voice, a small kindness) completely changed the meaning of something for you. What does that teach you about paying attention to small things?","placeholder":"A small detail that changed everything was…"},"further":{"id":"x1","type":"accent","title":"Challenge: harder words","intro":"Same task, harder words.","items":["teléfono","lección","jóvenes","exámenes","difícil","fútbol","pantalones","Perú"],"hints":["*Jóvenes* and *exámenes* are plurals. Count the syllables: the stress is now third-to-last."]},"culture":"**¿Sabías?** Capital letters can have accent marks too: **Á**, **É**, **Í**, **Ó**, **Ú**. The RAE says they should always be written, as in *ÁFRICA* or *Ángel*. Use the ⇧ key next to the accent keys to type a capital.","unitTitle":"¡Hola! Los fundamentos","code":"NDU3MS1FQVI=","prev":"m01-alfabeto.html","next":"m03-saludos.html"});
